    "https://wku.interviewexchange.com/jobofferdetails.jsp?JOBID=205077": {
      "title": "Trauma Clinician, LCCWEAR Integrated Family Trauma Treatment Clinic",
      "description": "Western Kentucky University\nJob Posting: Trauma Clinician, LCCWEAR Integrated Family Trauma Treatment Clinic Western Kentucky University \n\tJob Posting: Trauma Clinician, LCCWEAR Integrated Family Trauma Treatment Clinic Title Human Resources Subscribe to Email Alerts Returning Applicant Login Previous Next Careers > Search Jobs > Search Results > Job Details Trauma Clinician, LCCWEAR Integrated Family Trauma Treatment Clinic Category: Staff Subscribe: New Job Alerts RSS Job Feed Department: Dean CHHS Locations: Bowling Green, KY Posted: Sep 23, 2026 Closes: Open Until Filled Type: Full-time Ref. No.: 994708 Position ID: 205077 Share About Western Kentucky University: Western Kentucky University (WKU) is a student-centered, applied-research institution committed to providing its students with a transformative academic and social experience and to elevating its surrounding communities. Founded in 1906, WKU is the third largest public four-year institution in the Commonwealth of Kentucky with approximately 16,293 students and nearly 2,612 faculty and staff employees. Western Kentucky University is committed to empowering its campus community. At the heart of our mission, we seek to provide holistic education and employment experiences that prepare students, faculty, and staff to become effective scholars, contributors, and leaders in our ever-evolving communities. Located 65 miles north of Nashville, TN, and just over 100 miles south of Louisville, KY, WKU's main campus is in Bowling Green, KY - a city with approximately 70,000 residents. Our population is ethnically diverse, with more than 85 languages spoken in and around Bowling Green, and people from 35 nationalities call our community home. Western Kentucky University seeks to attract talented faculty and staff who are inspired individually to contribute to WKU's strategic plans and ongoing success. We offer comprehensive wellness and health benefits to employees and their families, including domestic partners and other qualified dependents and take pride in providing a workplace culture that supports balancing the responsibilities of work and family life. Paid-time off for holidays, vacation and sick leave, and a tuition discount program for employees and dependents are also provided. Learn more about WKU and Bowling Green at wku.edu/about . Job Description: WKU Center for Child Welfare is seeking applications for the Integrated Family Trauma Treatment Clinic (IFTTC) Trauma Clinician. The Trauma Clinician will provide evidence-based trauma therapy, collaborative services, and systemic advocacy for children, adults, and families from diverse backgrounds who are at risk of or who currently are involved with the child welfare system. Services will include therapy, comprehensive trauma assessments, ongoing reassessments and screening, education, and care coordination. The IFTTC evaluates the effectiveness of its interventions by tracking outcomes for participating children and families. Findings from this work will inform the continued development of a systemic intervention designed to strengthen child-caregiver relationships, promote permanency, and reduce the effects of trauma on parenting and family functioning. The Trauma Clinician must be able to establish priorities and work independently to meet deadlines. A qualified candidate will be organized, detail oriented, and have the capacity to work in a busy environment. The Trauma Clinician must provide services consistent with professional ethics and best-practice standards, while demonstrating integrity, accountability, and effective cooperation. The Trauma Clinician should possess proficiency in Microsoft Office applications, in addition to excellent listening, written, and verbal communication skills. The Trauma Clinician must possess strong decision-making and problem-solving skills and will report to the IFTTC Clinic Manager. While not exhaustive, the following duties are expected for this position. Duties may be altered based on client need or clinic need. Provide evidence-based or evidence-informed trauma-specific treatment to children and adults involved with the child welfare system. Collaborate and engage in collective advocacy with the child welfare and family court systems. Use measurement-based care, including validated, evidence-based clinical assessment and outcome measures, to guide treatment and monitor client progress. This position is based at the IFTTC's main office in Bowling Green, Kentucky. The clinician will also be required to travel to the clinic's satellite office in Glasgow, Kentucky, one day per week to provide services. Complete timely and accurate documentation of all services provided, including case notes and other required clinical and service-related records. Maintain an active caseload and provide a minimum of 15 direct client service hours per week (e.g., individual or group therapy; clinical assessments; caregiver sessions; and other approved face-to-face or telehealth clinical services). This position is supported through grant funding that is reviewed in two-year funding cycles. Continued employment is contingent upon funding and programmatic need. Requirements: Master's degree in social work, psychology, or clinical counseling Independent licensure in the state of Kentucky Additional Information: Salary Range:  $55,000 - $61,000 Kentucky state law requires all public institutions of postsecondary education to conduct pre-employment criminal background checks to determine suitability for employment. Western Kentucky University (WKU) is committed to a policy and practice of providing equal employment and educational opportunities to all individuals. In accordance with Title VI and VII of the Civil Rights Act of 1964, Title IX of the Educational Amendments of 1972, Section 504 of the Rehabilitation Act of 1973, Revised 1992, and the Americans with Disabilities Act of 1990, no form of discrimination or harassment will be tolerated at WKU on the basis of race, color, ethnicity, national origin, creed, religion, political belief, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity/expression, marital status, age, uniformed services, veteran status, genetic information, pregnancy, childbirth or related medical conditions, or physical or mental disability in admission to career and technical education programs and/or activities, or employment practices. Persons with disabilities, who need reasonable accommodations to participate in the application and/or selection process, may contact the Office of Institutional Equity at 270-745-5121 for assistance. To report concerns related to WKU's Discrimination and Harassment Policy, No. 0.2040 and/or WKU's Sex and Gender-Based Discrimination, Harassment, and Retaliation Policy, No. 0.2070, please complete the Discrimination, Harassment, and Retaliation Reporting Form . Additional information regarding Title IX, including contact information for the Title IX Coordinator and reporting options,  is available on WKU's Title IX website . Information concerning educational programs offered by WKU are provided at: https://www.wku.edu/academics/ For information related to job postings, application status, or the hiring process, please email employment@wku.edu . Application Instructions: The following items must be submitted through this electronic talent management system: Cover Letter Resume You will receive email confirmation that your submission has been received. The candidate review process will generally begin as materials are received. Frequently Asked Questions (FAQs) Powered by Hide Main Navigation Western Kentucky University 1906 College Heights Blvd. Bowling Green, KY 42101"
